 CHAPTER 1 - INTRODUCTION
1.1 Overview
Hospitals must manage patient details, doctor information, appointments and billing in a timely and accurate manner. When these activities are performed through paper registers or isolated files, staff spend more time searching, validating and calculating than serving patients. The Hospital Management System (HMS) is a browser-based application that centralizes these essential activities in one simple interface.
1.2 Background
The project recreates the functional flow of a desktop hospital application using standard web technologies. It intentionally uses a familiar Swing-inspired visual style: title bars, beveled buttons, table grids and confirmation dialogs. The result is a single HTML file that can be opened directly in a browser without installation, a build process or a server.
1.3 Need for the System
•	Fast access to patient and doctor information.
•	Reduction of manual calculation and transcription errors.
•	Consistent validation of age, phone number and charges.
•	A compact demonstration system suitable for a mini project.
CHAPTER 1 - INTRODUCTION (CONTINUED)
1.4 Problem Statement
A small hospital or clinic requires a straightforward way to register patients, maintain doctor details, schedule appointments and calculate bills. In a manual process, records may be duplicated, appointment details may be missed, and charges may be added incorrectly. The challenge is to provide an easy-to-use system that brings these operations together while remaining simple to deploy and understand.
1.5 Objectives
•	Authenticate the user before application modules are shown.
•	Register, select, update and delete patient records.
•	Maintain doctor details with specialization, fee and contact information.
•	Book and cancel appointments using valid patient and doctor records.
•	Generate itemized bills and compute totals automatically.
•	Give immediate confirmation and error messages for every important action.
1.6 Project Goal
The primary goal is to demonstrate complete front-end application development by combining user interface design, client-side validation, data handling and output presentation in one coherent healthcare workflow.
CHAPTER 1 - INTRODUCTION (CONTINUED)
1.7 Scope
The application is intended for a single administrator in a small, demonstration-scale hospital environment. It covers the first level of hospital administration: patient registration, doctor maintenance, appointment scheduling and billing. Each module is accessible from the taskbar and dashboard after a successful login.
1.8 Scope Boundaries
Included	Not Included
Administrator login	Multi-user / role-based accounts
Patient, doctor, appointment and bill records	Persistent cloud or database storage
Field validation and in-session updates	Clinical diagnosis or pharmacy inventory
Formatted receipt output	Online payment gateway
Browser-based standalone execution	Networked deployment and analytics
1.9 Benefits
The HMS improves record visibility, standardizes data entry, reduces arithmetic effort and makes the core hospital workflow easier to demonstrate. Since it runs locally in the browser, it is also highly portable and convenient for classroom evaluation.
CHAPTER 2 - SYSTEM ANALYSIS
2.1 Existing System
Traditional hospital administration in small settings often depends on notebooks, paper forms and separate spreadsheets. Patient information must be located manually. The availability of doctors and appointments may not be immediately known. Billing requires staff to add several amounts by hand, which increases the chance of calculation errors.
Issue	Effect on Operations
Manual record search	Delays in patient service
Repeated data entry	Duplicate or inconsistent records
Paper appointment register	Difficult appointment tracking
Manual arithmetic	Billing errors
No immediate validation	Invalid phone and age entries
These limitations indicate the need for a unified and interactive application. CHAPTER 2 - SYSTEM ANALYSIS (CONTINUED)
2.2 Proposed System
The proposed HMS provides a menu-driven browser interface. After login, the administrator moves between dashboard, patients, doctors, appointments and billing. JavaScript holds the current session data in arrays and refreshes the visible tables after every operation. Validation is performed before a record or bill is accepted.
