CHAPTER 3 - SYSTEM DESIGN
3.1 Architecture Design
The HMS uses a client-side architecture. The presentation layer provides windows, forms, tables and dialogs. The logic layer validates values and processes CRUD, appointments and bills. The in-memory store maintains arrays of patient, doctor, appointment and bill objects for the active browser session.
Figure 3.1: High-level architecture of the Hospital Management System
This architecture keeps the solution simple and makes each responsibility easy to trace during testing.

3.2 Use Case Design
The administrator is the single actor for the present version. Authentication is completed before any business operation is available. Once logged in, the user can manage records, book or cancel appointments and generate bills.
 
Figure 3.2: Use case diagram
Use Case Summary
Use Case	Outcome
Log in	Opens the protected application workspace.
Manage Patients / Doctors	Maintains the master data required by later modules.
Book Appointment	Creates a scheduled visit with patient, doctor, date and time.
Generate Bill	Produces an itemized receipt and calculated total.
3.3 Entity Relationship Model
Patient and doctor records provide the reference data for an appointment. A bill belongs to a patient and stores separate fee values before calculating the total. Identifiers ensure that records can be selected and updated consistently.
Figure 3.3: Simplified entity relationship model
Key Entities
Entity	Important Fields
Patient	id, name, age, gender, phone, address, disease
Doctor	id, name, specialization, fee, phone
Appointment	id, patient, doctor, date, time, status
Bill	id, patient, doctor fee, medicine, room, lab, total
3.4 Data Structure Design
The data store is initialized by freshStore(). It contains four arrays and four counters. The counters are padded to create readable identifiers such as P001 and D003. The selected patient, doctor and appointment identifiers are retained separately so that the correct row can be highlighted and updated.
Store Member	Purpose
patients	Array of patient objects registered during the session
doctors	Array of doctor objects; two sample doctors are loaded
appointments	Array of booked or cancelled appointment objects
bills	Array of generated bill objects
pC, dC, aC, bC	Counters for unique identifier generation
Data Integrity Rules
•	Age must be from 1 to 130.
•	Phone and contact values must contain exactly ten digits.
•	Date must follow dd-mm-yyyy format.
•	Amounts must be valid non-negative numbers. 3.5 User Interface Design
The UI is designed to look like a familiar desktop application while running in a web browser. A blue taskbar identifies the active module. The window chrome, buttons and table headers use bevel-style CSS. Forms align labels and controls, while grid rows can be selected for update or deletion.
UI Element	Design Purpose
Login window	Restricts access and shows login hints/errors.
Taskbar	Provides direct module navigation and exit action.
Dashboard tiles	Offers an icon-based overview of main functions.
Data grids	Shows rows and selection status for records.
Modal dialogs	Provides success, warning and confirmation messages.
Receipt panel	Uses monospaced text to present bill output.
This consistent visual treatment improves learnability by making controls behave similarly across all modules. 3.6 Process Logic
The application follows a simple control flow. Login validates the entered credentials. Screen switching hides non-selected modules. When a form is submitted, validation runs first; only valid data is saved. The related table or receipt is then rendered again. Confirmation dialogs prevent accidental destructive actions.
Process	Main Steps
Patient registration	Read fields - validate - assign ID - store - refresh table
Doctor search	Read keyword - filter doctor names - render matching rows
Appointment	Verify patient/doctor - validate date/time - store booking
Billing	Validate charges - calculate total - create receipt
Exit	Ask for confirmation - hide app - show restart screen
3.7 Error Handling
Errors are placed near the active form, making it clear which input must be corrected. Successful operations are communicated through a modal dialog, and delete or exit operations require an explicit Yes response.

CHAPTER 4 - IMPLEMENTATION
4.1 Development Environment
Component	Implementation
HTML5	Defines the login screen, application screens, forms, tables and dialogs.
CSS3	Creates the desktop-inspired theme, layout, title bars, buttons and table style.
JavaScript	Implements validation, navigation, data store, rendering and calculations.
Browser	Executes the application directly from the hms.html file.
4.2 Source Organization
The project is deliberately self-contained. The style sheet and JavaScript are embedded in one HTML document. This makes distribution simple and allows the project to run immediately when the file is opened in a browser. Functions are grouped by responsibility: login, screen switching, patients, doctors, appointments, billing and exit. 4.3 Login and Dashboard Module
The login module reads the username and password values. It rejects empty fields and accepts the configured administrator account. On success, it hides the login screen, shows the main application and renders the dashboard. The dashboard is a launch point for all functional modules.
 
Figure 4.1: Login interface
 
Figure 4.2: Dashboard navigation interface
4.4 Patient Management Module
The patient form collects name, age, gender, phone, address and disease. Validation prevents incomplete values, invalid age and incorrect phone length. Register creates a new patient object and refreshes the table. Clicking a row loads its data into the form, allowing update or delete actions.
 
Figure 4.3: Patient registration and record-management screen
4.5 Doctor Management Module
The doctor screen maintains name, specialization, consultation fee and contact number. Two sample doctors are included when the application starts. The module also supports searching by doctor name, which filters the table without changing the underlying records.
 
Figure 4.4: Doctor details and search screen
4.6 Appointment Module
The appointment form populates its patient and doctor lists from the available records. Booking checks that both references exist, validates the date and requires a time. Each successful booking receives an appointment ID and Booked status. A selected appointment can later be marked Cancelled.
 
Figure 4.5: Appointment booking screen
4.7 Billing Module
Billing selects a registered patient and accepts four charge heads: doctor fee, medicine charge, room charge and laboratory charge. Each value is checked as a non-negative number. The total is calculated automatically and shown in a formatted receipt panel, together with a confirmation dialog.
 
Figure 4.6: Bill generation and receipt screen
4.8 Validation and User Feedback
Validation functions are reused throughout the program. vNotEmpty verifies mandatory text, vPhone verifies ten digits, vAge checks the allowed age range, vDate checks the displayed date pattern and vNum verifies non-negative numerical values. This approach keeps the code consistent and avoids repeating validation logic.
Validation	Example Message
Empty patient name	Error: Name cannot be empty.
Invalid age	Error: Age must be valid (1-130).
Invalid phone	Error: Phone must be 10 digits.
Invalid bill value	Error: Doctor Fee must be a valid number.
No selected row	Error: Select a patient row first.
Modal feedback also confirms successful registration, update, booking, cancellation and billing.
This makes the system behaviour visible to the user at every important stage.

